    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Outcrop-scale 3-D fracture mapping and visualization provide quantitative means of recording and analyzing field observations. We use a \"Total Station\" to measure the relative Easting, Northing, and Elevation coordinates of the topographic surface and points along the peripheries of fractures preserved in an outcrop. These data are converted to a format compatible with the programMathematica. The fractures are idealized as polygonal surfaces and are viewed from arbitrary viewpoints with or without the topography. These observations could be combined with results from 3-D model calculations from quantitative structural geology boundary element or fluid flow tools.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Arrowsmith, Ramon, Myers, Rodrick and Lore, Jason. (1995). Outcrop-Scale 3-D Fracture Mapping and Visualization. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: http://purl.stanford.edu/bn833gg5897",
